Despite the unknown and complex motion of living creatures, the assumption
of an equal probability is in general of limited suitability, specifically
with one of the examined key sequences (see below). While the start of the
key sequence is inherently unknown, subsequent keys are correlated. Though
the resulting model is complex and maybe confusing, you must take geometric 
properties into account (paw size, horiz. and vert. distance between legs)
and movement dynamics (trajectory, cat's step geometry). There are no
independent single character events but character multiplets depending on
these geometric and dynamic factors! An F-R-D sequence, e.g., (possible by
an one paw step) is more likely than a O-T-Q sequence. In such a model with
correlated key events another keyboard layout topic will drop in; on an US
keyboard the sequence Alt-Q Y might be of different probability than on a
german keyboard (where the Y lies somewhere in between the Alt and Q key).

Summarized: The probabilities of the equal-distribution model are not
appropriate, and you don't need to know about the cat's preferences to
include necessary geometric key layout considerations.

> Next we need an estimate of how long a sequence of key signals was sent
> to the computer during the event.  I will assume that your cat held
> about 20 keys pressed at any moment and that she changed her position
> every tenth of a second, which means she sent 200 characters per second.

Hmm.., that value of 200 seems unrealistic too big to me. I've tried it
out and could not come close. But as a base to determine some magnitude
it may well be acceptable.

Hmm.., another thought; that number would be realistic (and then be even
much larger) if you would trigger the 'paste' keys or mouse button after
having copied much text to the clipboard. In this case there would be no
geometric factor but instead just the statistics of the pasted data (e.g.
english text with given character probabilities, including di- and
trigramm probabilities).

> [...]  Similarly, we can neglect the fact that the sequences
> "#qu<ret>y", "#qui<ret>y" and "#quit<ret>y" equally end the game as each
> of these needs at least one letter more, and each letter reduces the
> probability of the sequence by a factor of 100, so we are talking about
> modifications in the percent range and below.

Character sequences of adjacent keys like the "ui" in "#quit" are more
likely to be hit by one paw in one step. This sequence would not reduce
the probability by a magnitude of 100; you need coupled probabilities.
But since Alt-Q is a bigger threat than the #quit<ret> that multiplet is
in any case of minor importance.

> Now if you take into account that these are just rough estimates that
> can easily be off by a factor of 5 or even 10 (due to inevitable
> imponderabilities in the estimate), then the flat probability for this
> to happen when your cat hops onto your keyboard might be as high as 5%!
> Personally, I would draw the line here.  That is, my cat (if I had one)
> would have to stay out! ;-)

At this point I have to disagree. The value of 5% alone is of little use.
For the risk analysis you have to take into account the probability that
the cat will jump onto the keyboard during your game and the time played.

Since I have no cat I have no actual values :-) We just know about two(?)
such events in a ~60(?) sessions and ~155000 moves game. And most games
will last much shorter without any cat-event. If your strategy is to lock
out your cat you would have to do it every game; that's many hours! :-(
For me this event distribution would mean at most (on average) a single
cat-event in a complete ascended game (75000 moves). I would not lock out
my cat for so many hours of game playing just because of a small chance
during the potential but unlikely cat-event.

Making backups is always a better choice. ;-)

